        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Concrete does not need sealer to stay standing, but it often needs sealer to stay looking good and to get through cold winters without flaking. Seal exterior slabs that see freeze-thaw and salt, anything decorative, and any surface where stains matter. Leave plain concrete in a mild climate bare if you prefer; it will not fail because of it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you decide, give new concrete about 28 days or whatever the label says, do the water-drop test before you buy anything, and match the sealer to the job: penetrating for protection, film-forming for looks.
          </p>
        </div>

